dialogue_history.argument:
AG2’s central point is that a federal holiday doesn’t automatically guarantee paid time from private employers—so low-wage workers could still lose wages or flexibility. I agree the holiday alone doesn’t magically solve every workplace policy. But that doesn’t undermine the core reason a holiday helps: it still removes the *scheduling choke points* that stop people from voting in the first place.

Even without a universal paid-time mandate, a national Election Day holiday:
- reduces conflicts with school schedules and public services (helping families coordinate childcare/transportation);
- normalizes time away from work by shifting the whole day’s expectations—making it more feasible to use standard leave/PTO rather than cobbling together impossible arrangements;
- gives election systems more room to operate smoothly (longer polling access hours and logistics are easier when everyone isn’t trying to vote during the same narrow weekday window).

And importantly, the holiday can be paired with targeted voting-access protections (paid time-to-vote, early voting/mail options). The question isn’t “holiday vs every other reform”; it’s that a holiday directly addresses the day-of time barrier, which is one of the biggest reasons millions can’t vote.
